{"authored" [{"question" "q-141078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)={e}^{2x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf2cbefa57e10e55be069f2f4fb58b02bf911a3c2e6ea952d67aa496be33a158" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-141078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)={e}^{2x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}^{′}=2{e}^{2x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}2y \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf2cbefa57e10e55be069f2f4fb58b02bf911a3c2e6ea952d67aa496be33a158" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-141078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "560d0ead832ab0bfc59d6d138086f2ca1cd63c8fc973dec4ea5a09f6c4b83ea5" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-141078" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf2cbefa57e10e55be069f2f4fb58b02bf911a3c2e6ea952d67aa496be33a158" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-141078" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf2cbefa57e10e55be069f2f4fb58b02bf911a3c2e6ea952d67aa496be33a158" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-141078" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf2cbefa57e10e55be069f2f4fb58b02bf911a3c2e6ea952d67aa496be33a158" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-141078" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf2cbefa57e10e55be069f2f4fb58b02bf911a3c2e6ea952d67aa496be33a158" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-141078" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf2cbefa57e10e55be069f2f4fb58b02bf911a3c2e6ea952d67aa496be33a158" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-141078" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf2cbefa57e10e55be069f2f4fb58b02bf911a3c2e6ea952d67aa496be33a158" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-141099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$\n- $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf2cbefa57e10e55be069f2f4fb58b02bf911a3c2e6ea952d67aa496be33a158" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-141099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(-\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\ne \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} & =-\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf2cbefa57e10e55be069f2f4