 differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}}.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf2cbefa57e10e55be069f2f4fb58b02bf911a3c2e6ea952d67aa496be33a158" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-141103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-3})}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{x}^{-3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & ={x}^{3} \\\\ -1 & ={x}^{4}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3(-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-6})}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{x}^{-6}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & ={x}^{6} \\\\ -2 & ={x}^{7}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf2cbefa57e10e55be069f2f4fb58b02bf911a3c2e6ea952d67aa496be33a158" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-141103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "560d0ead832ab0bfc59d6d138086f2ca1cd63c8fc973dec4ea5a09f6c4b83ea5" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-141103" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio