le_sha256" "cf2cbefa57e10e55be069f2f4fb58b02bf911a3c2e6ea952d67aa496be33a158" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-352655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4y,\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{-4x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf2cbefa57e10e55be069f2f4fb58b02bf911a3c2e6ea952d67aa496be33a158" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-352655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that $y=A{e}^{-4x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(1)=2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(1)=2$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-4x} \\\\ 2 & =A{e}^{-4\\cdot 1} \\\\ 2 & =A{e}^{-4} \\\\ 2{e}^{4} & =A \\\\ A & =2{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf2cbefa57e10e55be069f2f4fb58b02bf911a3c2e6ea952d67aa496be33a158" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-352655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "560d0ead832ab0bfc59d6d138086f2ca1cd63c8fc973dec4ea5a09f6c4b83ea5" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-352655" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf2cbefa57e10e55be069f2f4fb58b02bf911a3c2e6ea952d67aa496be33a158" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-352655" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2e^{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf2cbefa57e10e55be069f2f4fb58b02bf911a3c2e6ea952d67aa496be33a158" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny' = -\\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad x \\neq 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$\n\n- Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y' = f'(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/example-525.json" "file_sha256" "7e6262d3b9fbec801f9e62963f1f8bea696e40f1ff58144a3e657894a2706bfa" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ and $y' = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/example-525.json" "file_sha256" "7e6262d3b9fbec801f9e62963f1f8bea696e40f1ff58144a3e657894a2706bfa" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-9978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y = e^x + 1$\n\n- $y = -e^x$\n\n- $y = 2e^x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/example-9978.json" "file_sha256" "9709d8252b399a20a35aa99ab1334ca59e76ed265a4cdc799d841dd8195fa672" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-9978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y = e^x + 1,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ {e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}{e}^{x}+1 \\\\ 0 & \\ne 1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y = e^x+1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = -e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ -{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = -e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = 2e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ 2{e}^{x} & =2{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = 2e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/example-9978.json" "file_sha256" "9709d8252b399a20a35aa99ab1334ca59e76ed265a4cdc799d841dd8195fa672" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8732" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{3y}{x}, \\qquad y(-1) = -2.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/example-8732.json" "file_sha256" "a82952d638b5574698aa2f0b2aba862f5a7e3f3ec32068ef1443ed21a2bde1e5" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8732" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1) = -2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}{-1}}) = {\\color{blue}{-2}}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{x}^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1)^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1) \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 2x^3 .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/example-8732.json" "file_sha256" "a82952d638b5574698aa2f0b2aba862f5a7e3f3ec32068ef1443ed21a2bde1e5" "basis" 879 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\frac{7}{x}$\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$\n- $y=\\frac{2}{x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "0caeb4df748e5794e42d96d62707cc18153268faa0437ec03b6cba299c55f0be" "basis" 1043 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{7}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{7}{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{2}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{2}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{2}{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "0caeb4df748e5794e42d96d62707cc18153268faa0437ec03b6cba299c55f0be" "basis" 1043 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "92219bf9aff9fd31ee772f9ff06a5327b98253daf41eab7da8acf82a11f302b6" "basis" 1043 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "0caeb4df748e5794e42d96d62707cc18153268faa0437ec03b6cba299c55f0be" "basis" 1043 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "0caeb4df748e5794e42d96d62707cc18153268faa0437ec03b6cba299c55f0be" "basis" 1043 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "0caeb4df748e5794e42d96d62707cc18153268faa0437ec03b6cba299c55f0be" "basis" 1043 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "0caeb4df748e5794e42d96d62707cc18153268faa0437ec03b6cba299c55f0be" "basis" 1043 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "0caeb4df748e5794e42d96d62707cc18153268faa0437ec03b6cba299c55f0be" "basis" 1043 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "0caeb4df748e5794e42d96d62707cc18153268faa0437ec03b6cba299c55f0be" "basis" 1043 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/state.json" "file_sha256" "0caeb4df748e5794e42d96d62707cc18153268faa0437ec03b6cba299c55f0be" "basis" 1043 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992289/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-141082" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141082" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141082" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bf1687b3e152e8ed56a58d17109f0feaa0ba75a8ba1a0a0abc7295d6074868b"} {"question" "q-141082" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141082" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141082" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)={e}^{2x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)={e}^{2x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}^{′}=2{e}^{2x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}2y \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bf1687b3e152e8ed56a58d17109f0feaa0ba75a8ba1a0a0abc7295d6074868b"} {"question" "q-141078" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141078" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141078" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141080" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141080" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\  & \\ne 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}2y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & \\ne \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141080" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bf1687b3e152e8ed56a58d17109f0feaa0ba75a8ba1a0a0abc7295d6074868b"} {"question" "q-141080" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141080" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141080" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141086" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3y\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=3{e}^{-x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=3{e}^{-x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141086" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=3{e}^{-x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(3{e}^{-x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne 3{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=3{e}^{-x}$ and ${y}^{′}=-3{e}^{-x}$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}-3y \\\\ -3{e}^{-x} & \\overset{?}{=}-3(3{e}^{-x}) \\\\ -3{e}^{-x} & \\ne -9{e}^{-x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=3{e}^{-x}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141086" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bf1687b3e152e8ed56a58d17109f0feaa0ba75a8ba1a0a0abc7295d6074868b"} {"question" "q-141086" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141086" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141086" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-140631" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{4y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)={x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=5{x}^{5}.$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-140631" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)={x}^{4},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{4})^{′} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3} \\\\  & \\ne 5{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y={x}^{4}$ and ${y}^{′}=4{x}^{3}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ (4{x}^{3}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4({x}^{4})}{x} \\\\ 4{x}^{3} & =4{x}^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y={x}^{4}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-140631" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bf1687b3e152e8ed56a58d17109f0feaa0ba75a8ba1a0a0abc7295d6074868b"} {"question" "q-140631" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-140631" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-140631" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}}.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{x}}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ 1 & =2\\sqrt{x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & =\\frac{2}{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-1/2})}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{x}^{-1/2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & =2\\sqrt{x} \\\\ -1 & =2x\\sqrt{x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bf1687b3e152e8ed56a58d17109f0feaa0ba75a8ba1a0a0abc7295d6074868b"} {"question" "q-141093" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141093" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141093" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-79335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y}{x}-2,\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=2x$\n- $y={x}^{2}$\n- $y={x}^{3}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-79335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=2x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot 2x}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}4-2 \\\\ 2 & =2.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=2x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{2},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot {x}^{2}}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}2x-2 \\\\ 0 & \\ne -2.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y={x}^{2}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{3},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot {x}^{3}}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}2{x}^{2}-2 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={x}^{3}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-79335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bf1687b3e152e8ed56a58d17109f0feaa0ba75a8ba1a0a0abc7295d6074868b"} {"question" "q-79335" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-79335" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-79335" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$\n- $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(-\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\ne \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} & =-\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bf1687b3e152e8ed56a58d17109f0feaa0ba75a8ba1a0a0abc7295d6074868b"} {"question" "q-141099" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141099" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141099" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-79333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\frac{7}{x}$\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$\n- $y=\\frac{2}{x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-79333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{7}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{7}{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{2}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{2}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{2}{x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-79333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bf1687b3e152e8ed56a58d17109f0feaa0ba75a8ba1a0a0abc7295d6074868b"} {"question" "q-79333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-79333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-79333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}}.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-3})}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{x}^{-3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & ={x}^{3} \\\\ -1 & ={x}^{4}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3(-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-6})}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{x}^{-6}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & ={x}^{6} \\\\ -2 & ={x}^{7}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bf1687b3e152e8ed56a58d17109f0feaa0ba75a8ba1a0a0abc7295d6074868b"} {"question" "q-141103" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141103" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141103" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2y,\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{-2x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that $y=A{e}^{-2x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(0)=2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(0)=2$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-2x} \\\\ 2 & =A{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ 2 & =A{e}^{0} \\\\ 2 & =A(1) \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2{e}^{-2x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bf1687b3e152e8ed56a58d17109f0feaa0ba75a8ba1a0a0abc7295d6074868b"} {"question" "q-141109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-352656" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y,\\,y(-1)=4.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-352656" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that $y=A{e}^{x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1)=4.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(-1)=4$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{x} \\\\ 4 & =A{e}^{-1} \\\\ 4 & =A{e}^{-1} \\\\ 4e & =A \\\\ A & =4e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-352656" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bf1687b3e152e8ed56a58d17109f0feaa0ba75a8ba1a0a0abc7295d6074868b"} {"question" "q-352656" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-352656" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4e"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141111" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡2x,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141111" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\sqrt{2}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141111" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bf1687b3e152e8ed56a58d17109f0feaa0ba75a8ba1a0a0abc7295d6074868b"} {"question" "q-141111" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141111" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141111" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-352655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4y,\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{-4x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-352655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that $y=A{e}^{-4x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(1)=2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(1)=2$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-4x} \\\\ 2 & =A{e}^{-4\\cdot 1} \\\\ 2 & =A{e}^{-4} \\\\ 2{e}^{4} & =A \\\\ A & =2{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-352655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bf1687b3e152e8ed56a58d17109f0feaa0ba75a8ba1a0a0abc7295d6074868b"} {"question" "q-352655" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-352655" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2e^4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141108" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{-x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141108" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that $y=A{e}^{-x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(0)=1$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-x} \\\\ 1 & =A{e}^{-0} \\\\ 1 & =A(1) \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=1{e}^{-x}={e}^{-x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141108" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bf1687b3e152e8ed56a58d17109f0feaa0ba75a8ba1a0a0abc7295d6074868b"} {"question" "q-141108" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141108" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "q-141108" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/state.json" "file_sha256" "c92fe05c0d79ecfea580028c49868da290f28034f0c7298c99214bff4f9a0aba"} {"question" "e-525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny' = -\\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad x \\neq 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$\n\n- Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y' = f'(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/example-525.json" "file_sha256" "7e6262d3b9fbec801f9e62963f1f8bea696e40f1ff58144a3e657894a2706bfa"} {"question" "e-525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ and $y' = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/example-525.json" "file_sha256" "7e6262d3b9fbec801f9e62963f1f8bea696e40f1ff58144a3e657894a2706bfa"} {"question" "e-9978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y = e^x + 1$\n\n- $y = -e^x$\n\n- $y = 2e^x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/example-9978.json" "file_sha256" "9709d8252b399a20a35aa99ab1334ca59e76ed265a4cdc799d841dd8195fa672"} {"question" "e-9978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y = e^x + 1,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ {e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}{e}^{x}+1 \\\\ 0 & \\ne 1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y = e^x+1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = -e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ -{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = -e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = 2e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ 2{e}^{x} & =2{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = 2e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/example-9978.json" "file_sha256" "9709d8252b399a20a35aa99ab1334ca59e76ed265a4cdc799d841dd8195fa672"} {"question" "e-8732" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{3y}{x}, \\qquad y(-1) = -2.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/example-8732.json" "file_sha256" "a82952d638b5574698aa2f0b2aba862f5a7e3f3ec32068ef1443ed21a2bde1e5"} {"question" "e-8732" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1) = -2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}{-1}}) = {\\color{blue}{-2}}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{x}^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1)^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1) \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 2x^3 .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041067/example-8732.json" "file_sha256" "a82952d638b5574698aa2f0b2aba862f5a7e3f3ec32068ef1443ed21a2bde1e5"}] "usage" {"e-9978" [] "e-525" [{"kind" "presentations" "entity" 17592186283106}] "q-140631" [] "q-141099" [] "q-79335" [] "e-8732" [] "q-79333" [] "q-141078" [] "q-352655" [] "q-141093" [] "q-141103" []} "basis" 1481 "retractions" [[17592186101068 :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$y^{\\prime } = \\frac{4y}{x}, x \\ne 0$\n\nLet $f(x) = x^{4}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f^{\\prime }(x) = 5x^{5}$.\n2. Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y^{\\prime } = f^{\\prime }(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n3. The function $y = f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation."] [17592186101068 :question/worked-solution "Differentiate $f(x)=x^4$: $f'(x)=4x^3$, so statement I is false. For $x\\ne0$,\n$$\\frac{4f(x)}x=\\frac{4x^4}x=4x^3=f'(x).$$\nThe substitution is an identity, so II and III are true. The answer is **II and III only**."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186098179 17592186098180 17592186098181 17592186098182 17592186098183 17592186098191 17592186098192 17592186098193 17592186098194 17592186098195 17592186100303 17592186100304 17592186100305 17592186100306 17592186100307 17592186281414 17592186281415 17592186281416 17592186281417 17592186281418 17592186281428 17592186281429 17592186281430 17592186281431 17592186281432 17592186281435 17592186281436 17592186281437 17592186281438 17592186281439 17592186281455 17592186283974 17592186283975 17592186283976 17592186283977 17592186283978] "immutable_fields" [[17592186098178 :answer-field/correct] [17592186098178 :answer-field/id] [17592186098178 :answer-field/key] [17592186098178 :answer-field/type] [17592186098190 :answer-field/correct] [17592186098190 :answer-field/id] [17592186098190 :answer-field/key] [17592186098190 :answer-field/type] [17592186100302 :answer-field/correct] [17592186100302 :answer-field/id] [17592186100302 :answer-field/key] [17592186100302 :answer-field/type] [17592186281413 :answer-field/correct] [17592186281413 :answer-field/id] [17592186281413 :answer-field/key] [17592186281413 :answer-field/type] [17592186281427 :answer-field/correct] [17592186281427 :answer-field/id] [17592186281427 :answer-field/key] [17592186281427 :answer-field/type] [17592186281434 :answer-field/correct] [17592186281434 :answer-field/id] [17592186281434 :answer-field/key] [17592186281434 :answer-field/type] [17592186281454 :answer-field/correct] [17592186281454 :answer-field/id] [17592186281454 :answer-field/key] [17592186281454 :answer-field/type] [17592186283973 :answer-field/correct] [17592186283973 :answer-field/id] [17592186283973 :answer-field/key] [17592186283973 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
